VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — Two-class method for participating securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Glenwood Industries has net income of $800,000, 200,000 weighted-average common shares, and 50,000 participating preferred shares entitled to equal per-share participation with common stock after a $2 per share annual preference. No dividends were declared. Under the two-class method, what is basic EPS attributable to common shareholders?', '["$3.50", "$4.00", "$2.80", "$3.20"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, participating securities require the two-class method for undistributed earnings. Preferred preference = 50,000 x $2 = $100,000. Remaining undistributed = $700,000, allocated proportionally: common share = $700,000 x (200,000/250,000) = $560,000. Basic EPS = $560,000 / 200,000 = $2.80. Choice B ($4.00) is incorrect because it ignores the participation feature entirely and computes $800,000 / 200,000.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Definition of simple capital structure
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which best describes a simple capital structure?', '["A structure with only common stock and no potentially dilutive securities outstanding", "A structure with fewer than 1,000 shareholders of record", "A structure where all debt is short-term and unsecured", "A structure with no more than two classes of equity"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, a simple capital structure exists when an entity has no potentially dilutive securities such as options, warrants, or convertible instruments outstanding. Only basic EPS is required because diluted EPS would be identical to basic EPS. Choice D is incorrect because multiple equity classes can exist in a simple structure as long as none are potentially dilutive.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Retroactive adjustment of prior-period EPS after stock split
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakfield Corp. reported basic EPS of $5.00 for Year 1 based on net income of $500,000 and 100,000 weighted-average shares. In January Year 2, before the Year 1 financial statements are issued, Oakfield declares a 2-for-1 stock split. What is the effect on Year 1 basic EPS as reported in the Year 2 comparative financial statements?', '["Year 1 EPS remains at $5.00 with a footnote disclosure only", "Year 1 EPS is retroactively restated to $2.50 to reflect the stock split", "Year 1 EPS is restated to $10.00 because the split doubles equity", "No adjustment is required since the split occurred in Year 2"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits occurring after the reporting period but before financial statements are issued require retroactive adjustment of all prior-period EPS amounts. Adjusted shares = 100,000 x 2 = 200,000. Restated EPS = $500,000 / 200,000 = $2.50. Choice A is incorrect because a footnote alone is insufficient; the EPS amounts themselves must be restated on the face of the income statement.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Convertible bonds mid-year if-converted method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hawthorne Corp. has net income of $500,000 and 100,000 weighted-average common shares. On July 1, the company issued $1,000,000 face value, 6% convertible bonds, each $1,000 bond converting into 20 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. Using the if-converted method, what is diluted EPS?', '["$5.00", "$4.82", "$4.35", "$4.75"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, convertible bonds issued mid-year are time-weighted. Interest saved = $1,000,000 x 6% x 6/12 = $30,000. Tax effect = $30,000 x 25% = $7,500. After-tax add-back = $22,500. Incremental shares = 1,000 bonds x 20 shares x 6/12 = 10,000. Diluted EPS = ($500,000 + $22,500) / (100,000 + 10,000) = $522,500 / 110,000 = $4.75. Choice B ($4.82) is incorrect because it omits the tax adjustment, adding back the full $30,000 pretax interest.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — EPS presentation for discontinued operations
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinecrest Industries reports income from continuing operations of $720,000 and a loss from discontinued operations (net of tax) of $180,000, producing net income of $540,000. Weighted-average shares are 120,000. If the company presents only EPS for net income ($4.50), what presentation requirement under ASC 260 is violated?', '["ASC 260 requires EPS for each component of other comprehensive income", "ASC 260 requires EPS for income from continuing operations in addition to net income", "ASC 260 requires EPS only for net income, so no requirement is violated", "ASC 260 requires separate EPS for discontinued operations on the face of the income statement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, an entity must present basic and diluted EPS on the face of the income statement for both income from continuing operations and net income. EPS for discontinued operations may be presented on the face or in the notes but is not mandatory on the face. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 260 explicitly requires EPS for continuing operations whenever discontinued operations exist.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Definition of complex capital structure
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what type of entity has a complex capital structure?', '["An entity with potentially dilutive securities outstanding, such as stock options or convertible bonds", "An entity with more than $10 million in total assets", "An entity with operations in multiple countries", "An entity that reports other comprehensive income"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-3, a complex capital structure exists when an entity has outstanding securities that could potentially dilute basic EPS, including stock options, warrants, convertible debt, and convertible preferred stock. Such entities must present both basic and diluted EPS. Choice B is incorrect because asset size has no bearing on capital structure classification for EPS purposes.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — Dual presentation requirement (basic and diluted)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what EPS amounts must an entity with a complex capital structure present on the face of the income statement?', '["Only diluted EPS is required", "Only basic EPS, with diluted EPS in the notes", "Basic EPS, diluted EPS, and adjusted EPS", "Both basic EPS and diluted EPS with equal prominence"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, entities with complex capital structures must present both basic and diluted EPS with equal prominence on the face of the income statement for all periods presented. Neither figure may be relegated to the notes alone. Choice A is incorrect because basic EPS must always be presented alongside diluted EPS, not omitted.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — Written put options and reverse treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Crestview Industries wrote put options obligating the company to repurchase 10,000 shares at $50 per share when the average market price is $40 per share. The CFO argues these options should be excluded from diluted EPS because they are out-of-the-money from the option holder''s perspective. Evaluate whether the CFO''s conclusion is correct under ASC 260.', '["The CFO is incorrect; written put options use the reverse treasury stock method, and since the exercise price ($50) exceeds market price ($40), they are dilutive", "The CFO is correct; written put options are always excluded from diluted EPS", "The CFO is incorrect; written put options are included using the standard treasury stock method at market price", "The CFO is correct; only purchased put options affect diluted EPS calculations"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options that are in-the-money (exercise price exceeds market price) require the reverse treasury stock method. The entity assumes it issues enough shares at market ($40) to fund repurchasing shares at the exercise price ($50), producing 2,500 net incremental shares (12,500 issued minus 10,000 repurchased). Choice B is incorrect because in-the-money written puts are always dilutive and must be included.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: hard / L3 — Options granted mid-year require time-weighting
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Summitview Corp. has net income of $800,000, 200,000 weighted-average common shares, and no preferred stock. On April 1, the company grants 30,000 stock options with an exercise price of $20 when the average market price for the remaining period is $50. The controller calculates 18,000 incremental shares for the full year using the treasury stock method. Analyze whether this calculation is correct.', '["The calculation is correct; options are included for the full year regardless of grant date", "The calculation is incorrect; incremental shares should be only 12,000 because repurchased shares are the relevant figure", "The calculation is incorrect; the 18,000 incremental shares must be time-weighted for 9 months, yielding 13,500 shares", "The calculation is incorrect; stock options should use the if-converted method rather than the treasury stock method"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, stock options granted during the period are included in diluted EPS only from the grant date forward. The treasury stock method yields 18,000 incremental shares (30,000 options minus 12,000 repurchased at $50), but these must be time-weighted for 9/12 of the year = 13,500 shares. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 requires time-weighting from the grant or issuance date.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Which entities must present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which entities are required to present earnings per share on the face of the income statement?', '["All entities that prepare financial statements under U.S. GAAP", "Public entities with common stock or potential common stock traded in a public market", "Only entities with revenues exceeding $100 million annually", "Only entities registered with the SEC as large accelerated filers"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required only for public business entities whose common stock or potential common stock is traded in a public market. Private companies are not required to present EPS, though they may voluntarily do so. Choice A is incorrect because private entities preparing GAAP financial statements are exempt from the EPS presentation requirement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Definition of potentially dilutive security
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which of the following is an example of a potentially dilutive security?', '["A noncumulative preferred stock that does not convert to common stock", "A term loan payable to a commercial bank", "Treasury stock held by the issuing company", "A stock option giving the holder the right to purchase common shares"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-16, potentially dilutive securities include stock options, warrants, convertible debt, and convertible preferred stock because they could result in additional common shares being issued upon exercise or conversion. Choice A is incorrect because nonconvertible preferred stock cannot generate additional common shares and therefore has no dilutive potential.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Effect of stock dividend on previously reported EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ferndale Corp. reported basic EPS of $4.00 for Year 1 based on net income of $800,000 and 200,000 weighted-average shares. In March Year 2, before the financial statements are issued, Ferndale declares a 25% stock dividend. Analyze the effect on Year 1 basic EPS as reported in the Year 2 comparative financial statements.', '["Year 1 basic EPS remains at $4.00 with disclosure of the subsequent stock dividend", "Year 1 basic EPS is restated to $5.00 because the dividend increases equity", "Year 1 basic EPS is retroactively restated to $3.20", "Year 1 basic EPS is restated to $3.00 reflecting a one-third increase in shares"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends require retroactive restatement of EPS for all periods presented. Adjusted shares = 200,000 x 1.25 = 250,000. Restated EPS = $800,000 / 250,000 = $3.20. Choice D ($3.00) is incorrect because it treats the 25% stock dividend as a one-third increase in shares, confusing the dividend percentage with the proportional relationship.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Diluted EPS with RSUs via treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Maplewood Corp. has net income of $620,000 and 150,000 weighted-average common shares. The company has 10,000 restricted stock units (RSUs) outstanding for the full year. The average unrecognized compensation cost is $200,000, and the average market price is $40 per share. Under the treasury stock method, what is diluted EPS?', '["$4.00", "$4.13", "$3.88", "$4.43"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28, RSUs are included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method, with unrecognized compensation cost treated as assumed proceeds. Proceeds = $200,000. Shares repurchased = $200,000 / $40 = 5,000. Incremental shares = 10,000 - 5,000 = 5,000. Diluted EPS = $620,000 / 155,000 = $4.00. While Choice B ($4.13) represents basic EPS ($620,000 / 150,000), it is incorrect because it ignores the RSU dilution entirely.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Evaluate antidilutive convertible bonds exclusion
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Westbrook Holdings has basic EPS of $3.00 based on net income of $800,000, $50,000 in preferred dividends, and 250,000 weighted-average shares. The company has $500,000 face value, 10% convertible bonds, each $1,000 bond converting into 8 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. The CFO proposes including the bonds in diluted EPS. Evaluate whether the bonds should be included.', '["The bonds should be included because all convertible securities must appear in diluted EPS", "The bonds should be excluded because their per-share impact of $9.38 exceeds basic EPS of $3.00, making them antidilutive", "The bonds should be excluded because bond interest is not tax-deductible for EPS purposes", "The bonds should be included because the per-share impact exceeds basic EPS, which increases diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-17, potentially dilutive securities are excluded from diluted EPS if inclusion would be antidilutive. The bonds'' per-share impact = ($500,000 x 10% x 0.75) / (500 x 8) = $37,500 / 4,000 = $9.38, which exceeds basic EPS of $3.00. Including them would increase diluted EPS above basic EPS. Choice A is incorrect because antidilutive securities must always be excluded from diluted EPS.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Treasury stock method applies to options and warrants
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which method is used to calculate the dilutive effect of stock options and warrants on diluted EPS?', '["The if-converted method", "The effective interest method", "The treasury stock method", "The equity method"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method is used for stock options and warrants. It assumes the proceeds from exercise are used to repurchase common shares at the average market price, and only net incremental shares are added to the denominator. Choice A is incorrect because the if-converted method applies to convertible securities such as convertible bonds and convertible preferred stock, not options.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Mandatorily convertible preferred effect on basic vs diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stonegate Corp. has net income of $500,000, 100,000 weighted-average common shares, and 10,000 shares of mandatorily convertible preferred stock that will convert into 25,000 common shares on January 1, Year 3. Preferred dividends declared were $50,000 for Year 1. Analyze how the mandatorily convertible preferred affects basic and diluted EPS.', '["Basic EPS is $5.00 and diluted EPS is $4.00 because mandatorily convertible preferred dividends are never deducted", "Basic EPS and diluted EPS are both $4.00 because mandatory conversion requires including shares in both calculations", "Both basic and diluted EPS are $4.50 because the conversion does not occur until Year 3", "Basic EPS is $4.50 and diluted EPS is $4.00 because dividends are deducted for basic but added back for diluted under the if-converted method"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40 and 45-44, mandatorily convertible preferred stock is treated like other convertible preferred for EPS purposes. Basic EPS = ($500,000 - $50,000) / 100,000 = $4.50 (dividends deducted). Diluted EPS uses if-converted: $500,000 / (100,000 + 25,000) = $4.00 (dividends added back, converted shares included). Choice B is incorrect because mandatory conversion does not change the basic EPS calculation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: easy / L2 — Weighted-average shares with issuance and repurchase
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Cedarbrook Inc. had 80,000 shares outstanding on January 1. On April 1, it issued 40,000 shares for cash. On October 1, it repurchased 20,000 shares. What is the weighted-average number of common shares for the year?', '["80,000", "120,000", "105,000", "100,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, weighted-average shares account for the time each share count was outstanding. January through March: 80,000 x 3/12 = 20,000. April through September: 120,000 x 6/12 = 60,000. October through December: 100,000 x 3/12 = 25,000. Total = 105,000. Choice D (100,000) is incorrect because it uses the year-end balance without time-weighting the different periods.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Diluted EPS with warrants using treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Birchfield Corp. has net income of $558,000, 180,000 weighted-average common shares, and no preferred stock. The company has 15,000 warrants outstanding for the full year with an exercise price of $24 per share. The average market price is $40 per share. What is diluted EPS?', '["$3.10", "$3.00", "$2.86", "$3.20"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, warrants use the treasury stock method. Proceeds = 15,000 x $24 = $360,000. Shares repurchased at market = $360,000 / $40 = 9,000. Incremental shares = 15,000 - 9,000 = 6,000. Diluted EPS = $558,000 / (180,000 + 6,000) = $558,000 / 186,000 = $3.00. While Choice A ($3.10) represents basic EPS ($558,000 / 180,000), it is incorrect because it ignores the warrant dilution entirely.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Evaluate interim period EPS computation approach
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Goldcrest Corp. reports net income of $240,000 for Q2 with 100,000 weighted-average shares. The company has 20,000 stock options with an exercise price of $15. The Q2 average market price is $30, and the year-to-date average market price is $25. The controller uses the $25 year-to-date average to compute Q2 diluted EPS. Evaluate whether this approach is correct under ASC 260.', '["The approach is correct; year-to-date average market price should be used for consistency across periods", "The approach is correct if the company applies the method consistently across all quarters", "The approach is incorrect; the year-end closing stock price should be used for all interim periods", "The approach is incorrect; ASC 260 requires using each individual quarter''s average market price for that quarter''s incremental share calculation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, diluted EPS for each interim period uses the average market price during that specific period for the treasury stock method. Using Q2''s $30 average yields 10,000 incremental shares (20,000 minus $300,000/$30), while the incorrect $25 average yields only 8,000 incremental shares (20,000 minus $300,000/$25). Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 does not permit substituting the year-to-date average.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — EPS when preferred dividends exceed net income
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornfield Technologies reports net income of $120,000, 80,000 weighted-average common shares, and $180,000 in cumulative preferred dividends. What is basic EPS?', '["$(0.75)", "$0.00", "$1.50", "$0.75"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, cumulative preferred dividends are deducted from net income regardless of whether declared. Basic EPS = ($120,000 - $180,000) / 80,000 = $(60,000) / 80,000 = $(0.75). EPS can be negative when preferred dividend claims exceed net income available to common shareholders. Choice B ($0.00) is incorrect because basic EPS is not floored at zero; the full shortfall must be reported.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Analyze in-the-money vs out-of-the-money options
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redwood Inc. has two sets of stock options: Set A consists of 10,000 options with an exercise price of $20, and Set B consists of 8,000 options with an exercise price of $55. The average market price during the year is $50. Analyze the combined effect of both sets on diluted EPS.', '["Both sets are dilutive, adding a combined 8,800 incremental shares to the denominator", "Neither set affects diluted EPS because options are not potentially dilutive securities", "Only Set A is dilutive, adding 6,000 incremental shares; Set B is excluded because its exercise price exceeds market price", "Only Set B is dilutive because its higher exercise price generates more assumed proceeds"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, stock options are dilutive only when the exercise price is below the average market price (in-the-money). Set A: proceeds = $200,000, shares repurchased = 4,000, incremental = 6,000 (dilutive). Set B: exercise price of $55 exceeds the $50 market price, making it out-of-the-money and antidilutive. Choice A is incorrect because antidilutive Set B options must be excluded from diluted EPS.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Compute basic EPS with mid-year share issuance
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Lakeshore Manufacturing had 200,000 shares outstanding on January 1 and issued 40,000 additional shares on July 1. Net income is $660,000 with no preferred dividends. What is basic EPS?', '["$3.30", "$3.00", "$2.75", "$3.67"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the 40,000 shares issued on July 1 are weighted for 6/12 of the year. Weighted-average shares = 200,000 + (40,000 x 6/12) = 220,000. Basic EPS = $660,000 / 220,000 = $3.00. Choice A ($3.30) is incorrect because it uses only the beginning-of-year share count ($660,000 / 200,000) rather than reflecting the mid-year issuance in the weighted average.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Where EPS must be presented
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, where must earnings per share amounts be presented?', '["In the notes to the financial statements only", "On the balance sheet", "On the face of the income statement", "In the statement of cash flows"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, basic and diluted EPS must be presented on the face of the income statement for all periods for which an income statement is presented. EPS may not be relegated to the notes alone. Choice A is incorrect because footnote-only presentation does not satisfy the face-of-income-statement requirement mandated by ASC 260.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — If-converted method definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which method is used to determine the dilutive effect of convertible bonds on diluted EPS?', '["The treasury stock method", "The weighted-average method", "The cost recovery method", "The if-converted method"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method is used for convertible securities such as convertible bonds and convertible preferred stock. It assumes conversion occurred at the beginning of the period (or issuance date if later), adds back interest net of tax to the numerator, and includes converted shares in the denominator. Choice A is incorrect because the treasury stock method applies to options and warrants, not convertible instruments.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Rights issue bonus element effect on EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Sterling Corp. offers a rights issue to existing shareholders: one new share at $15 for every five shares held. The market price before the rights issue is $30 per share with 500,000 shares outstanding. Analyze the effect of the bonus element on weighted-average shares for EPS purposes.', '["Pre-rights-issue weighted-average shares are multiplied by an adjustment factor of approximately 1.09 to account for the bonus element", "No adjustment is needed because the rights issue price is below market value", "Only the new shares from the rights issue are added to the weighted-average calculation without any adjustment", "The bonus element requires reducing the EPS denominator to offset the dilutive effect"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-20, a rights issue below market price contains a bonus element requiring an adjustment factor. Theoretical ex-rights price = (5 x $30 + 1 x $15) / 6 = $27.50. Factor = $30 / $27.50 = 1.09. Pre-rights-issue shares are multiplied by 1.09 to reflect the bonus element before adding new shares from the rights date forward. Choice B is incorrect because a below-market rights issue inherently contains a bonus element requiring adjustment.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Compute diluted EPS with convertible preferred stock
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ashland Corp. has net income of $700,000 and 175,000 weighted-average shares. It has 5,000 shares of $80 par, 5% convertible preferred stock, each share converting into 6 common shares. Preferred dividends declared total $20,000. What is diluted EPS?', '["$3.89", "$3.41", "$4.00", "$3.50"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, convertible preferred stock uses the if-converted method. Per-share impact = $20,000 / 30,000 shares = $0.67, which is below basic EPS of $3.89, so the conversion is dilutive. Diluted EPS = $700,000 / (175,000 + 30,000) = $700,000 / 205,000 = $3.41. Although Choice A ($3.89) represents basic EPS, it is incorrect because it does not assume conversion of the preferred stock into common shares.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Evaluate contingently issuable shares with unmet conditions
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Evergreen Dynamics has an earn-out agreement from a prior acquisition: 50,000 additional shares will be issued if cumulative EBITDA exceeds $5,000,000 over three years. At the end of Year 1, cumulative EBITDA is $1,200,000. The CFO included the 50,000 shares in diluted EPS, arguing EBITDA will likely exceed the threshold by Year 3. Evaluate whether this treatment is correct.', '["The treatment is incorrect; contingently issuable shares are included in diluted EPS only if all conditions are satisfied as of the reporting date, and $1,200,000 does not meet the threshold", "The treatment is correct; management''s reasonable expectation of meeting the target justifies inclusion", "The treatment is incorrect; contingently issuable shares are never included in diluted EPS under any circumstances", "The treatment is correct; all earn-out shares must be included in diluted EPS from the acquisition date forward"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in diluted EPS only if all necessary conditions would be satisfied assuming the end of the reporting period were the end of the contingency period. Since $1,200,000 cumulative EBITDA does not meet the $5,000,000 threshold, the shares are excluded. Choice B is incorrect because management''s forecast cannot override the as-of-reporting-date test prescribed by ASC 260.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Analyze effect of share buyback on basic EPS trend
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Northridge Corp. had net income of $1,000,000 in both Year 1 and Year 2. In Year 1, weighted-average shares were 250,000. During Year 2, the company repurchased 50,000 shares on July 1, reducing weighted-average shares to 225,000. Analyze the effect of the buyback on the Year 1 to Year 2 basic EPS trend.', '["Basic EPS remains at $4.00 because the buyback has no effect on EPS calculations", "Basic EPS decreases because the share repurchase reduces total stockholders'' equity", "Basic EPS increases from $4.00 to $4.44 because the buyback reduced weighted-average shares while net income stayed constant", "Basic EPS increases to $5.00 because the repurchased shares are removed for the entire year"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, repurchased shares are removed from the weighted-average from the repurchase date forward. Year 1 EPS = $1,000,000 / 250,000 = $4.00. Year 2 EPS = $1,000,000 / 225,000 = $4.44. The buyback mechanically increases EPS by reducing the denominator while income remains constant. Choice D ($5.00) incorrectly removes all 50,000 shares for the full year rather than time-weighting from July 1.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Noncumulative preferred with undeclared dividends
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Clearwater Partners has net income of $360,000, 90,000 weighted-average common shares, and 10,000 shares of $50 par, 4% noncumulative preferred stock. No dividends were declared during the year. What is basic EPS?', '["$4.00", "$3.78", "$3.60", "$3.56"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, only declared dividends on noncumulative preferred stock are deducted from net income for basic EPS. Since no dividends were declared, the full $360,000 is available to common shareholders. Basic EPS = $360,000 / 90,000 = $4.00. Choice B ($3.78) is incorrect because it deducts $20,000 of undeclared dividends (10,000 x $50 x 4%), which is required only for cumulative preferred stock, not noncumulative.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Evaluate complex capital structure with multiple securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Crestline Corp. has net income of $840,000, 200,000 weighted-average shares, and $40,000 in convertible preferred dividends, yielding basic EPS of $4.00. The company also has: (1) 20,000 stock options (exercise $25, market $50) yielding 10,000 incremental shares; (2) $300,000 face, 8% convertible bonds, each $1,000 bond converting into 20 shares (tax rate 25%), with after-tax interest of $18,000 and 6,000 incremental shares; and (3) convertible preferred stock converting into 8,000 shares. Evaluate which securities to include and the proper dilution sequence.', '["Include only the options; exclude both bonds and preferred stock as antidilutive", "Include all three securities because they are all potentially dilutive convertible instruments", "Include options and preferred; exclude bonds because their $3.00 per-share impact is too high", "Include options and bonds; exclude preferred because its $5.00 per-share impact exceeds running EPS, making it antidilutive"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, securities are ranked by per-share impact from most to least dilutive. Options ($0 per share) first: $800,000 / 210,000 = $3.81. Next, bonds ($18,000 / 6,000 = $3.00): $3.00 < $3.81, so dilutive; running EPS = $818,000 / 216,000 = $3.79. Test preferred ($40,000 / 8,000 = $5.00): $5.00 > $3.79, antidilutive — exclude. Diluted EPS = $3.79. Choice B is incorrect because the convertible preferred is antidilutive and must be excluded.', 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;